#: ../../../how-to/configure-hardware-backed-disk-encryption.md:0
msgid ""
"Set or change a disk encryption PIN or passphrase, replace the recovery key "
"and manage recovery keys with snap-tpmctl on TPM/FDE systems."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to/configure-hardware-backed-disk-encryption.md:7
msgid "Topics: Installation Security"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to/configure-hardware-backed-disk-encryption.md:11
msgid "Configure hardware-backed disk encryption"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to/configure-hardware-backed-disk-encryption.md:13
msgid ""
"On an Ubuntu installation where the hardware-backed disk encryption (TPM/"
"FDE) is enabled, you can configure certain security options. You can also "
"recover access to your disk in certain scenarios."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to/configure-hardware-backed-disk-encryption.md:17
msgid "Set a disk encryption PIN or passphrase"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to/configure-hardware-backed-disk-encryption.md:19
msgid ""
"For an additional layer of security, you can add an encryption PIN or "
"passphrase. You enter them every time your computer starts up to unlock the "
"disk. A PIN or passphrase is also required on systems that don't provide a "
"hardware root of trust, though you can disable it afterward."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to/configure-hardware-backed-disk-encryption.md:21
msgid ""
"To learn when you might want to enable the PIN or passphrase, see {ref}`tpm-"
"fde-encryption-passphrase`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to/configure-hardware-backed-disk-encryption.md:0
msgid "Graphical interface"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to/configure-hardware-backed-disk-encryption.md:27
msgid "Open the Security Center app."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to/configure-hardware-backed-disk-encryption.md:28
msgid "Go to the Disk Encryption tab."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to/configure-hardware-backed-disk-encryption.md:29
msgid "Click {guilabel}`Add PIN` or {guilabel}`Add passphrase`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to/configure-hardware-backed-disk-encryption.md:30
msgid "Enter a numeric PIN or an alphanumeric passphrase and confirm."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to/configure-hardware-backed-disk-encryption.md:0
msgid "Command line"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to/configure-hardware-backed-disk-encryption.md:35
#: ../../../how-to/configure-hardware-backed-disk-encryption.md:99
msgid "Make sure that the `snap-tpmctl` tool is installed:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to/configure-hardware-backed-disk-encryption.md:45
msgid "Add a PIN or passphrase:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to/configure-hardware-backed-disk-encryption.md:47
msgid "PIN:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to/configure-hardware-backed-disk-encryption.md:57
msgid "Passphrase:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to/configure-hardware-backed-disk-encryption.md:67
msgid ""
"Later, you can change your PIN or passphrase using the `replace-pin` and "
"`replace-passphrase` subcommands."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to/configure-hardware-backed-disk-encryption.md:70
msgid ""
"The next time you reboot, your system asks for your PIN or passphrase. "
"Alternatively, you can also enter the disk recovery key if you forget your "
"PIN or passphrase."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to/configure-hardware-backed-disk-encryption.md:74
msgid "Get a new recovery key"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to/configure-hardware-backed-disk-encryption.md:76
msgid ""
"Ubuntu Desktop shows you your disk recovery key right after installation. If "
"you lose your recovery key, replace it as soon as possible. Otherwise, you "
"risk losing access to your data. You can't retrieve the existing recovery "
"key but you can get a new one."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to/configure-hardware-backed-disk-encryption.md:78
msgid ""
"To get the recovery key, you must be logged into your Ubuntu user account."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to/configure-hardware-backed-disk-encryption.md:81
msgid ""
"If you can't log in, you have no way to get a new recovery key. In that "
"case, follow {ref}`tpm-fde-no-recovery-key`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to/configure-hardware-backed-disk-encryption.md:84
msgid ""
"You need to be an administrator on your system to replace the recovery key."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to/configure-hardware-backed-disk-encryption.md:91
msgid "Go to the {menuselection}`Security Center --> Disk Encryption`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to/configure-hardware-backed-disk-encryption.md:92
msgid "Select {guilabel}`Replace recovery key…`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to/configure-hardware-backed-disk-encryption.md:93
msgid ""
"The Security Center displays your new recovery key. The previous recovery "
"key stops working as soon as you select {guilabel}`Replace`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to/configure-hardware-backed-disk-encryption.md:94
msgid ""
"Store your new recovery key somewhere safe, such as in a password manager."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to/configure-hardware-backed-disk-encryption.md:109
msgid "List the recovery keys on your system:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to/configure-hardware-backed-disk-encryption.md:-1
msgid ""
"Recovery Keys:\n"
"* default-recovery"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to/configure-hardware-backed-disk-encryption.md:122
msgid ""
"Usually, only one recovery key is active, and it's called `default-"
"recovery`. In an enterprise environment or a custom setup, you might find a "
"secondary recovery key."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to/configure-hardware-backed-disk-encryption.md:124
msgid "Replace the recovery key:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to/configure-hardware-backed-disk-encryption.md:134
msgid "Replace `default-recovery` with the name of your recovery key."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to/configure-hardware-backed-disk-encryption.md:136
msgid "The command displays your new recovery key."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to/configure-hardware-backed-disk-encryption.md:138
msgid "Store it somewhere safe, such as in a password manager."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to/configure-hardware-backed-disk-encryption.md:142
msgid "Manage recovery keys for an organization"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to/configure-hardware-backed-disk-encryption.md:144
msgid ""
"You can centrally manage the recovery keys for multiple systems encrypted "
"with TPM/FDE. This is useful if you're the system administrator at an "
"organization where you need centralized control over all encrypted systems "
"(\"escrow\"). In this setup, TPM/FDE adds a secondary recovery key on each "
"system. This secondary key is subject to central management."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to/configure-hardware-backed-disk-encryption.md:147
msgid ""
"If the local user of the managed system has root privileges, they can modify "
"both recovery keys on their system using the `snap-tpmctl` tool. The "
"Security Center only gives access to the primary, local recovery key."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to/configure-hardware-backed-disk-encryption.md:150
msgid ""
"Recovery key management is integrated into the **Landscape** systems "
"administration tool. For details, refer to the [Landscape documentation]"
"(https://documentation.ubuntu.com/landscape/)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to/configure-hardware-backed-disk-encryption.md:152
msgid ""
"Alternatively, you can build a custom central management solution based on "
"the interfaces provided by the `snap-tpmctl` tool:"
msgstr ""

